    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Lesson 2.1: Particle Swarm Optimization\n",
        "$_\\text{Metaheuristics and Optimization | D.J.D. Lopez | 2026}$\n",
        "\n",
        "Particle swarm optimization (PSO) is a **population-based** metaheuristic inspired by the collective motion of bird flocks and fish schools, where a swarm of particles cooperatively searches for good solutions in a continuous space. Each particle represents a candidate solution with a position and a velocity, and the algorithm iteratively updates these quantities so that particles both explore new regions and exploit information discovered by the swarm.\n",
        "\n",
        "At the heart of PSO is the velocity update, which combines three influences on each particle’s motion: inertia, a cognitive component, and a social component. For particle $i$ at iteration $t$, the standard PSO equations are  \n",
        "$$\n",
        "v_i^{t+1} = \\omega v_i^{t} + c_1 r_1 (p_i - x_i^{t}) + c_2 r_2 (g - x_i^{t})\n",
        "$$  \n",
        "$$\n",
        "x_i^{t+1} = x_i^{t} + v_i^{t+1}\n",
        "$$  \n",
        "Here $\\omega$ is the inertia weight, $c_1$ and $c_2$ are the cognitive and social coefficients, $r_1, r_2 \\sim U(0,1)$ are random numbers, $p_i$ is the personal best position of particle $i$, and $g$ is the best position found by the swarm (or a neighborhood). The inertia term carries momentum from the current velocity, the cognitive term pulls the particle toward its own best-known position, and the social term pulls it toward the swarm's best-known position, with randomness ensuring that particles stochastically sample around promising regions.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Because PSO treats the objective as a black box and only requires function evaluations, it can be adapted to many optimization problems, including operations-research (OR) models such as integer linear programs or mixed-integer nonlinear programs. In this notebook, we will see how PSO can be integrated into OR workflows by encoding decision variables as particles, enforcing constraints through penalties or repair strategies, and combining PSO with exact solvers (for example, CVXPY-based MILP or MINLP models) in hybrid pipelines."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "GIoN2JeYHzq0"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 1. Creating the Base Model (MILP)\n",
        "\n",
        "We first introduce a mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) formulation of the travelling salesman problem (TSP) that we will use as a running example throughout the notebook. We will first give a brief overview of TSP, its decision variables, and its core constraints, then build a concrete MILP model that can be solved using CVXPY. After that, we will empirically explore how the computational effort grows as we increase the number of cities, highlighting the combinatorial nature and practical scaling limits of exact TSP solvers."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pUt86d_OI_Yp"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### 1.1 Travelling Salesman Problem (TSP)\n",
        "\n",
        "The travelling salesman problem (TSP) asks for the shortest possible tour that visits each city in a given set exactly once and then returns to the starting city. It is typically modeled on a complete graph where nodes represent cities and edges represent travel legs, each with an associated distance or cost. TSP is a classic NP-hard problem in combinatorial optimization, which means that the number of possible tours grows factorially with the number of cities and exact solution methods can become computationally expensive as instances get larger.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Parameters**\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "* Set of cities: $N = \\{1, \\dots, n\\}$.\n",
        "*  Distance (or cost) between cities $i$ and $j$: $c_{ij} \\ge 0$ for all $i \\ne j$.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**Decision Variables**\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "* $x_{ij} \\in \\{0,1\\}$ for all $i \\ne j$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$ x_{ij} =\n",
        "    \\begin{cases}\n",
        "        1, & \\text{if the tour travels directly from city } i \\text{ to city } j, \\\\\\\\\n",
        "        0, & \\text{otherwise.}\n",
        "    \\end{cases} $$\n",
        "* (MTZ formulation) $u_i$ for $i \\in N$ are continuous or integer *order* variables used to eliminate subtours.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "**Objective Function**\n",
        "\n",
        "Minimize the total travel cost:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\min \\sum_{i \\in N} \\sum_{\\substack{j \\in N \\\\ j \\ne i}} c_{ij} x_{ij}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Degree Constraints**\n",
        "\n",
        "Each city has exactly one outgoing and one incoming arc:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_{\\substack{j \\in N \\\\ j \\ne i}} x_{ij} = 1 \\quad \\forall i \\in N,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_{\\substack{j \\in N \\\\ j \\ne i}} x_{ji} = 1 \\quad \\forall i \\in N.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Subtour Elimination Constraints (MTZ)**\n",
        "\n",
        "To eliminate subtours, introduce MTZ constraints on $u_i$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "u_i - u_j + n\\, x_{ij} \\le n - 1 \\quad \\forall i \\ne j,\\ i,j \\in N,\\ i \\ge 2,\\ j \\ge 2,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "with bounds\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1 \\le u_i \\le n-1 \\quad \\forall i \\in N,\\ i \\ge 2,\\quad u_1 = 0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "These constraints ensure that the $u_i$ values define a consistent visiting order, which is only possible for a single Hamiltonian tour and thus prevents subtours."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ayF0nOrVJzaB"
      }
    },
