    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 2. Implementing PSO\n",
        "\n",
        "This section introduces how to use MEALPY to implement particle swarm optimization and connect it to our TSP model. We will first give a brief overview of the MEALPY library and how it represents optimization problems and PSO algorithms. Then we will build a custom PSO routine for TSP by defining a suitable solution encoding, fitness function, and PSO hyperparameters, and finally apply this routine to the same traveling salesman instances used in our CVXPY experiments."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "zgxwrIFWJP8w"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### 2.1. MealPy\n",
        "\n",
        "MEALPY is a Python library that implements a wide range of metaheuristic optimizers, including several variants of particle swarm optimization, in a unified interface. In MEALPY, you define an optimization problem by specifying a fitness function, lower and upper bounds, and the optimization direction (“min” or “max”), then choose an algorithm class (such as a PSO variant) and call its `solve` method to search for a good solution. The official documentation, including a simple getting-started guide and advanced usage notes, is available at: https://mealpy.readthedocs.io."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "U6B_9AnSJR_p"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### 2.2. Instantiating PSO\n",
        "\n",
        "In MEALPY, a PSO run has two main parts: defining the problem and instantiating a PSO model.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, you define the problem as a dictionary with at least a fitness function, lower and upper bounds, and the optimization direction:\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "8C0MrqdQJe_f"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "from mealpy import PSO, FloatVar, IntegerVar\n",
        "\n",
        "def objval(solution):\n",
        "    x = solution[0]\n",
        "    return x**4 - 3*x**3 + 2\n",
        "\n",
        "bounds = FloatVar(lb=[-5.0], ub=[5.0])\n",
        "\n",
        "problem = {\n",
        "    \"obj_func\": objval,\n",
        "    \"bounds\": bounds,\n",
        "    \"minmax\": \"min\",\n",
        "}\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7bOHagw2RqDR"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Then you instantiate a PSO variant (for example, `OriginalPSO`) by specifying the number of iterations (`epoch`), swarm size (`pop_size`), and PSO hyperparameters like cognitive and social weights:"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yoo762SVRydu"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "model = PSO.OriginalPSO(\n",
        "    epoch=10,       # number of iterations\n",
