    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "For small TSP instances, PSO and the exact CVXPY model often produce very similar tour costs, so the objective gap stays near zero. As the number of cities grows, PSO's stochastic search tends to miss the exact optimum more often, and the gap (PSO - CVXPY) widens.\n",
        "\n",
        "PSO's runtime grows with problem size because each extra city enlarges the search space and makes every fitness evaluation more expensive, but this growth is usually smoother and more controllable (via epochs and population size). Exact MILP solvers face an NP-hard search space, so their solve times can increase very sharply and unpredictably as $n$ grows, even if they are fast on small instances.\n",
        "\n",
        "In practice, PSO is useful when you want reasonably good tours quickly and can accept some suboptimality. Exact solvers are preferred when you need guaranteed optimal solutions or tight bounds, but they may be unsuitable for time-critical settings as problem size grows because of their rapidly increasing solving time."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Z7UqgDctf6XA"
      }
